{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S9mhjm5LtAgu6qX8W3UGn9","type":"tool_result","content":"Online: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050314] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050314].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:14\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback \n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n\n------\n124451 PLAN.md\n# CRACKMANIA — build plan\n\n**Title: CRACKMANIA** (\"crack the clock\"). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"TrackMania\" were brainstormed, then\none was drawn with an OS-entropy RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` over the list → index 8 → *Crackmania*).\nThe name works three ways and the game leans on all of them: a *crack* driver (an expert), *cracking* a track's\nclock, and the puzzle mode's *cracks* (gaps in a track you fill with blocks). The finale track is called \"The Big\nCrack\" (a jump over a canyon).\n\nThis plan is for the developers (possibly other AI models) who will build the game from TASK.md and this file alone.\nRead sections 0–3 whole before writing code; sections 4–12 and the appendices are the reference you build each\nsystem from.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Deliverables, names and ground rules\n\n| What | Exact name / place |\n| --- | --- |\n| Game title (title screen, About, hub tags) | `CRACKMANIA` (display), `Crackmania` (file-safe) |\n| SOURCE token in file names | `TrackMania2003` |\n| Dev folder (all source, tools, tests) | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810/` in the folder that holds TASK.md and PLAN.md |\n| The single-file game | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810.html` in that same root folder (beside TASK.md) |\n| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` in the root, beside the HTML (shape in TASK.md; must parse as JSON) |\n| Engine source | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, commit `4390a98` (v0.85.2): `dist/handoff/` (2D engine, docs, tools) **plus** `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` and `vendor/` (the 3D layer; see §2.2) |\n| AI Hub client | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub`, `lib/hub/` at commit `f8835e4` (protocol 1, client 1.4.0) |\n\nGround rules from TASK.md that shape every decision:\n- Everything is drawn and synthesized by code (no image or sound files). Voices are Animalese-style gibberish.\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in the shipped game. Every feature listed here ships working.\n- Other agents share this machine: work only inside the dev folder (plus a scratch dir of your own), never kill\n  processes you did not start, bind servers to `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind\n  127.0.0.1`), and stop them by PID. During planning `/tmp` (a 9.8 GB tmpfs) was **full**; put clones, npm caches and\n  build output on the main disk (e.g. `npm_config_cache=<devfolder>/.npm-cache`, `TMPDIR=<devfolder>/.tmp`).\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§9). Screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n- Final chat reply and the in-game About/Credits screen carry Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and\n  Prompt Feedback (TASK.md lists the fields; \"Plan\" = `PLAN.md`). Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n**Pitch.** An original arcade time-attack stunt racer in the spirit of TrackMania (Nadeo, PC, 21 Nov 2003). Short\ntracks built from blocks (loops, kickers, boost pads, banked bends), a clock you race against, a ghost of your best\nrun, medals, coppers to spend on track pieces, and an editor where every track — yours or ours — is just blocks on a\ngrid. Three environments with their own car and handling. Original names, art, music, tracks and characters.\n\n**What the source is (research done for this plan).** TrackMania (2003): developer Nadeo; publishers Focus Home\nInteractive (FR), Digital Jesters (UK), Enlight (US, June 2004); director Florent Castelnérac; music Henri-Pierre\nPellegrin. Solo modes **Race, Puzzle and Survival** (Survival — three computer ghost cars, finish last and you're out,\nfirst or second ranks you up — arrived with the Power Up! update), each with tracks in every environment; three\nenvironments **Desert, Rally (countryside) and Snow (alpine)**, each with its own car (muscle \"speed\" cars, rally\nsuper-minis, alpine 4x4s); a **track editor** whose grid-snapped blocks (\"bricks\": roads, checkpoints, long bends,\nloop-the-loops, jumps) are **bought with coppers** earned from bronze/silver/gold medals; a **car painter**;\nmultiplayer by **hot seat, LAN and Internet**; tracks and skins shared online. Metacritic 74. TrackMania's DNA, which\nthis game must capture: *the clock is the enemy*, *instant respawn/restart*, *ghosts and checkpoint splits*, *stunts\nare the route*, *tracks are toys anyone builds*, *terse, playful presentation*.\n\n**Genre:** arcade racing (time attack) + track-building puzzle. **Perspective:** third-person chase camera (far\nchase, close chase, hood cam) in races; an orbiting three-quarter overhead camera over a 3D grid in the editor and\npuzzles; trackside TV cameras in replays.\n\n**Look:** the engine's native style — low-resolution 3D (the game's 320x240-class buffer, `expand: true`), toon\nshading with ink outlines on code-drawn parts, crisp nearest-filtered pixel textures baked by code, PS1/N64-era\nracer. Bright TrackMania colours: red/white curbs, chunky gates with flip-digit time boards, chevron boost pads.\n\n**Modes (all ship):**\n1. **Race** — 15 tracks (5 per environment), medals Bronze/Silver/Gold/Author, coppers, unlocks.\n2. **Puzzle** — 6 puzzles: start, checkpoints and finish are placed; you get a limited block inventory; build a route,\n   then drive it. Faster routes earn better medals.\n3. **Survival** — per environment, a 5-round gauntlet against three ghost rivals (the \"Ghost Guild\"); last place is\n   out, 1st/2nd rank you up.\n4. **Build** — the track editor (place/rotate/raise/remove blocks, coppers cost, undo/redo, test drive, validate by\n   driving, 6 save slots, share codes).\n5. **Hot Seat** — 2–8 players take turns on one controller/keyboard; standings over a cup of tracks.\n6. **Split Screen** — 2–4 local players at once (pads or keyboard halves); cars are ghosts to each other.\n7. **Online** — AI Hub sessions in the hub's `ghost` mode: everyone time-attacks the same track for 3 minutes and sees\n   the others as ghost cars (TrackMania's","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"133e450b-8b13-41c9-9125-2ea73f490cb8","uuid":"a3f70bde-5cae-4c27-99a1-12f84bedb88e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:29.449Z","tool_use_