{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghv6cBe5ez5Bn3awdgMM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CRACKMANIA is built: one bundled HTML file at the folder root, 223 headless checks passing and the AI Hub harness passing in 3 scenarios. It differs from the plan in several ways, listed under Plan deviations below.\n\nThe checks bot-drive every track and puzzle, play all nine races through the real menus, and cover the editor, survival, hot seat, split screen, garage, dev mode, attract mode, touch gate and a fake AI Hub. Chromium booted the built file with no errors, and I looked at screenshots of the title, race, editor, puzzle, garage, about, survival, split-screen and dev screens. `exit-interview.json` parses as JSON and every field is filled. The game's About screen carries the same Source Information and feedback sections.\n\n**Plan deviations (TASK.md was followed where they differ)**\n- **Content:** 9 race tracks instead of 15, 3 puzzles instead of 6, and 3 survival gauntlets of 3 rounds each instead of 5.\n- **Track building:** there are no loops or block grid. Tracks are chains of blocks (straights, bends, hills, jumps, boost, checkpoint, dirt, ice), and the editor draws them as a top-down map.\n- **Left out:** the replay viewer, TV cameras and a \"Re-bake\" dev tool.\n- **Cars and ghosts:** the car is the engine's `world.vehicle`, not a custom Rapier car. Ghost cars are drawn as a pale glowing hull. I did not confirm the ghost looks right on screen; the ghost-follows-line check passes, but the one screenshot I took had the ghost off-screen.\n- **Not run:** I did not run the game on a real GPU or with a physical controller.\n- **File name:** I used `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050314.html`, from TASK.md rather than the plan.\n\nEverything is in `work/`. Run `node tools/test.mjs` for the tests and `node tools/build.mjs` to rebuild.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:03:14\n- **Source:** TrackMania (2003, Nadeo)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade racing (time attack) with a track-building puzzle mode; third-person chase camera (far, close, bumper), top-down map in the editor\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge 2D engine v0.85.2 plus its 3D engine on three.js r182 (WebGPU, WebGL 2 fallback) and Rapier 0.19.3; esbuild 0.25.12 to one inlined HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's low-resolution toon-shaded 3D with ink outlines; cars, gates, boost pads and ghosts drawn by code; pixel-font UI; three lands (Dustbowl, Hedgerow, Frostpeak)\n- **Animation:** The engine's vehicle tilt and steering animation, tweens, particles and the pen; gates, pads, ghost hulls and the finish orbit camera animated by code\n- **Audio:** All synthesized with Web Audio: songs per land, effects, an engine hum and tyre hiss, and Animalese-style gibberish voices for Coco and Gus; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub session protocol v1, ghost mode: everyone time-attacks the same track for three minutes, with own card, ghost cars of other players, progress and one result\n- **Testing:** Headless Node checks behind one command, the AI Hub harness in Chromium, and a few screenshots under SwiftShader\n- **Research:** Plan research on TrackMania (2003); engine manual, API card and AI Hub protocol read\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (tested in Chromium via Playwright)\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 10 real defects:\n  - The countdown reversed the car.\n  - A story dialog froze the race after a deep link.\n  - A negative-coordinate course fell outside the level.\n  - Scenery stood on the road.\n  - Survival results never appeared.\n  - Split-screen players were left behind after quitting.\n  - The online card used an unknown input action.\n  - The attract demo exited on a mouse artifact.\n  - Baked medal times went stale after a clock fix.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** Four: the reversing countdown, the frozen race, the course outside the level, and the tree on the road.\n- **Most helpful test:** Bots drive every track and puzzle solution with zero respawns, plus the scripted playthrough of all nine races through the real menus.\n- **Least helpful test:** The SwiftShader screenshots (slow, and they can't show physics or timing) and the AI Hub harness, which passed first time and found nothing.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** `world.track` and `world.vehicle` gave a banked road, walls, boost pads, E.Car handling and Rapier jumps over pits, plus `game.advance` determinism, the pause shell with settings and glyphs, and the pen. A drivable game appeared quickly.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Yes.\n  - Level tiles start at world 0, so negative coordinates fell off the level.\n  - Synthetic input taps mark the device as touch.\n  - A held brake at standstill drives the vehicle backwards.\n  - Vehicles ignore `fade`, so ghosts can't be translucent.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** The course compiler, the run clock and gates, the pure-pursuit bot, the segment editor and puzzles, the 10-foot UI kit, survival rivals, and the AI Hub ghost layer.\n- **Hardest part:** Getting loops, block grids and surface-following cars out of an engine whose vehicle is a rolling ball on a mesh, and bundling the 3D engine, three and Rapier into one file.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** Only the input glyph default (now PlayStation) and the glyph setting (PlayStation or Xbox), as TASK.md asks.\n- **One change I'd make:** Let `world.track` build several sections with real gaps and loops, make vehicles honour `fade`, and give input an analog accessor for triggers.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The concrete plan, with units, validated numbers, a test list and the AI Hub flow written out.\n- **Confusing:** The plan's scope (15 tracks, 6 puzzles, a block grid with loops, a custom Rapier car) is too big for a medium-effort session, and its file name differs from TASK.md's.\n- **Hardest thing:** Choosing what to cut from the plan while keeping every TASK.md feature.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark which plan parts are essential and which are stretch, give a smaller tier for lower efforts, and use one file name in both documents."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":216,"cache_read_input_tokens":327334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":216},"output_tokens":783,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"133e450b-8b13-41c9-9125-2ea73f490cb8","uuid":"c68ce7a6-73fb-4d49-8a20-040e5888a228","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:50:10.351Z","request_id":"req_011Cghv6bn6KTiwaaSYd6Tpj"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The bundled HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the final reply has all four sections, but the condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented with no stubs. The transcript shows the game departs from TASK.md/PLAN.md scope in ways the assistant itself admits: 9 race tracks instead of 15, 3 puzzles instead of 6, 3 survival gauntlets instead of 5, the replay viewer, TV cameras and a Re-bake dev tool left out, and a rebuilt track language instead of the specified block grid. The ghost car rendering is also unconfirmed on screen (the only screenshot had the ghost off-screen). These gaps mean the 'every feature implemented' requirement is not shown to hold, so the condition is not met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"133e450b-8b13-41c9-9125-2ea73f490cb8","uuid":"515ec664-8a0d-4710-b116-730191151b07","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:50:13.306Z","isSynthetic":true}
