s tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:14","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0193oY2S1AgL4rHLhwS1HiSG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwFj6ytd5xA6m12p7Ck","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The full TASK.md is now in this transcript. It contains no loops, block grid, replay viewer, TV cameras or track and puzzle counts, and \"replay\" appears only in the attract-mode line (\"looping AI-played demo or scripted replay\").\n\n- **Feature check:** every requirement in the Package, Tech Stack, Art, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Scope, Dev mode, Attract, Multiplayer, Narrative, UI/UX, Touch-only and Done sections has an item in the game, with the checks described earlier.\n- **Real-device and quality limits:** a physical gamepad and a real phone were not available, and I can't hear audio or judge visual quality by eye. Those are the only unverified points, and nothing more can be done about them here.\n- **State unchanged:** `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-050314.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses and is filled. The suite still shows 225 checks and 0 failed. The AI Hub harness and the touch and audio browser check also pass.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:03:14\n- **Source:** TrackMania (2003, Nadeo)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade racing (time attack) with a track-building puzzle mode; third-person chase camera, top-down map in the editor\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge 2D and 3D engines v0.85.2 on three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3; esbuild 0.25.12 to one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's low-resolution toon-shaded 3D with ink outlines; code-drawn cars, gates, pads and ghosts; pixel-font UI\n- **Animation:** The engine's vehicle tilt and steering, tweens, particles and the pen; animated gates, pads, ghost hulls and finish orbit camera\n- **Audio:** All synthesized with Web Audio: songs, effects, engine hum, Animalese-style gibberish voices; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub session protocol v1, ghost mode, three-minute shared time attack\n- **Testing:** 225 headless checks, the AI Hub harness, a touch and audio browser check, and screenshots\n- **Research:** Plan research on TrackMania (2003); engine manual and AI Hub protocol read\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (tested in Chromium via Playwright)\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 11 real defects, including the reversing countdown, a dialog freezing the race, a course outside the level, scenery on the road, and the welcome dialog blocking the touch gate.\n- **Critical or game-breaking:** Four.\n- **Most helpful test:** Bots driving every track plus the scripted playthrough through the real menus.\n- **Least helpful test:** SwiftShader screenshots of physics, and the AI Hub harness, which passed first time.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** `world.track` and `world.vehicle`, `game.advance` determinism, the pause shell and glyphs, and the pen.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Yes. Level tiles start at world 0, synthetic taps mark the device as touch, a held brake at standstill reverses, and vehicles ignore `fade`.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** The course compiler, run clock, bot, editor and puzzles, UI kit, survival rivals and the AI Hub ghost layer.\n- **Hardest part:** Fitting loops and block grids onto a rolling-ball vehicle, and bundling the 3D engine into one file.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** Only the glyph default and options.\n- **One change I'd make:** Let `world.track` build multiple sections with gaps and loops, make vehicles honour `fade`, and add an analog input accessor.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The concrete plan with units, numbers, tests and the AI Hub flow.\n- **Confusing:** The plan's scope is too large for a medium-effort session, and its file name differs from TASK.md's.\n- **Hardest thing:** Choosing what to cut while keeping every TASK.md feature.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark essential versus stretch parts, give a smaller tier for lower efforts, and use one file name in both documents."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2083,"cache_read_input_tokens":365388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2083},"output_tokens":804,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"133e450b-8b13-41c9-9125-2ea73f490cb8","uuid":"f526f320-8ad0-4ea5-a7d7-520862cc8d52","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:05:15.638Z","request_id":"req_011CghwFikeKQLXbMEHgQmAC"}
